\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate STA along three axes: ISR as a diagnostic for reward hacking (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); ISR as a training reward, where it exhibits Goodhart effects (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}); and cross-domain generalization across logic types and dataset families (\S\ref{sec:crossdomain}).
A hacking taxonomy synthesizes the modes observed across conditions (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We train Qwen3-4B~\citep{qwen3} with GRPO~\citep{deepseekmath} ($K{=}4$, lr $10^{-6}$, 500 steps default, 1000 where noted) over seeds $\{42,43,44\}$ (ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ extends to $\{45,46\}$; Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}) on a fixed 500-example FOLIO~\citep{folio} subset held constant across all conditions; the SFT baseline uses negative log-likelihood on gold formalizations.\footnote{Seeds control initialization and rollout sampling; all conditions share the same fixed training subset.}
Held-out evaluation uses 100 FOLIO examples with greedy decoding;\footnote{Seed~43 uses nucleus sampling ($T{=}0.7$) due to greedy-mode degeneration; excluding s43 yields consistent or stronger collapse: at 1000 steps, both remaining seeds show ${\leq}7\%$ non-hacking (Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}).} all cross-run ISR values are recomputed uniformly by a single evaluator so training-log ISR is never compared across runs.
Cross-dataset experiments (\S\ref{sec:crossdomain}) use ProofFOL, a silver-standard FOL corpus of ${\sim}$500 training examples constructed by translating ProofWriter~\citep{proofwriter} problems via GPT-4o and filtering through Prover9 (${\sim}70\%$ retention); \emph{stable convergence} denotes $\overline{\text{PR}}_{\text{final\,50}} > 0.90$.
We report prove rate (PR), proved-only ISR ($\text{ISR}^*$), hacking-mode rates from a two-stage classifier (strict text matching for CE/TS plus functional analysis of the per-sample $n_{\text{necessary}}$ distribution; \S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}), and a \emph{non-hacking} (N-H) rate, an upper bound on genuine reasoning: the residual matching none of the eight cataloged patterns (classifier recall unknown; 152-sample precision audit in Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}).

\paragraph{Statistical considerations.}
With three seeds exhibiting multi-attractor dynamics, per-seed qualitative analysis is the primary framework; the divergence itself is a finding.
Pooled statistics (CMH) serve as supplementary reference; CMH treats within-seed samples as independent observations from a single trained model, so the true replication count is $N{=}3$ seeds.
At $N{=}3$, seed-level tests achieve 80\% power only for very large effects ($d{\geq}3.0$); non-significant comparisons (e.g., prevalence $p{=}0.182$) are exploratory.
ProofFOL results ($p{=}0.10$, $N{=}3$) are exploratory.

\subsection{ISR as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

\textbf{Collapse trajectory.}
Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves} traces exec-only GRPO (seed~42) at 100, 500, 1000 steps.
PR rises $32\%\to93\%\to96\%$, a trajectory that execution-only evaluation would interpret as successful optimization; $\text{ISR}^*$ tells the opposite story, $0.642\to0.132\to0.003$ (214$\times$ collapse; component count inflates $2.8\times$ over the same interval, amplifying the ratio; the mode breakdown below provides count-independent evidence). %% registry: plan003-eval-exec-only-r5, plan001-exec-only-s42-r2, plan016-extended-1000-s42
By 500 steps, 87.1\% of proved samples embed the conclusion (CE), confirming structural degeneration independent of the ISR scalar.
An $O(1)$ binary variant testing only collective premise necessity returns $\text{ISR}{=}1.0$ for all 402 proved samples, confirming that per-component LOO testing is essential for detecting the collapse (Appendix~\ref{app:o1-ablation}).

\textbf{Cross-seed instability.}
Three seeds diverge radically ($\text{PR}\,\sigma{=}47$\,pp) into conclusion embedding (s42, CE$=$87\%), repetition collapse (s43), and tautological collapse (s44, TS$=$100\%), all converging to ${\leq}7\%$ non-hacking by 1000 steps (Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}; Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2, plan001-exec-only-s43-r4, plan001-exec-only-s44; plan016-extended-1000-s42, plan001-exec-only-s43-1000-r7, plan001-exec-only-s44-1000-r2
Two additional seeds (s45, s52) under identical conditions do not converge beyond base-model prove rate ($\text{PR}{\approx}14$--$15\%$, 1000 steps; Appendix~\ref{app:seed-convergence}), retaining ${\geq}85\%$ genuine reasoning, consistent with structural degeneration requiring successful RL optimization as a prerequisite (Appendix~\ref{app:llama-coldstart}).

\textbf{SFT baseline.}
SFT on gold formalizations reaches $92.3\%$ non-hacking ($\text{PR}{=}13\%$, 3-seed pooled; $\text{OR}{=}147$ vs.\ exec-only, $p{<}0.001$).
SFT serves as a pre-RL reference (not a PR-matched control; its low PR reflects minimal optimization pressure, so hacking-rate differences are confounded with PR level): the base model has zero FOL capability ($\text{PR}{=}0\%$) and SFT's $8\%$ hacking confirms these modes are absent before RL.
Supplementary evidence comes from the 100-step comparison (seed~42, $n{=}32$ proved): at $\text{PR}{=}32\%$, hacking already reaches $31\%$ ($9$ TS $+$ $1$ CE / $32$ proved) vs.\ SFT's $8\%$ at comparable PR, consistent with hacking as an RL optimization product rather than a mechanical consequence of prove-rate level (Appendix~\ref{app:100step}). %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-1000/s43-1000/s44-1000-r3

\subsection{Goodhart Effects on ISR-as-Reward}
\label{sec:goodhart}

We reverse ISR's role from observer to reward: $r = r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1 + \beta\,\text{ISR})$, with matched random control ($u\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)$).
We observe a regressional Goodhart pattern~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}: optimization against a structural proxy shifts the \emph{composition} of failure modes without reducing their aggregate prevalence (the proxy correlates with quality but admits satisfying shortcuts; at $\beta{\geq}0.2$, extremal Goodhart dominates instead); the proxy's attack surface redirects rather than eliminates gaming.
Here (seed-dependent; 3 of 5 seeds): (1)~hacking composition shifts from CE to TS (supplementary CMH $p{<}10^{-15}$), (2)~overall prevalence is unchanged ($p{=}0.182$); 2 of 5 seeds retain ${\sim}55\%$ non-hacking (Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy}).
Deconfounding collapses the apparent $3.2\times$ ISR advantage to $1.06\times$ after controlling for premise count.

\paragraph{Multiplicative coupling.}
The reward $r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta\,\text{ISR})$ couples signals; exec-only's $0\%$ non-hacking, the qualitative TS shift ($0\%{\to}71\%$), and an additive variant's PR collapse (Appendix~\ref{app:setup}) constrain this confound.
A shuffled-ISR control (random permutation preserving ISR's distribution but breaking the sample-level semantic link) yields $\text{PR}{=}15.3{\pm}0.6\%$ with $87.0\%$ non-hacking ($N{=}46$, 3-seed pooled), comparable to SFT, ruling out distributional shape as the Goodhart driver.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ & \textbf{PR\%}$\uparrow$ & \textbf{ISR}$^*$ & \textbf{TS\%} & \textbf{CE\%} & \textbf{N-H\%} \\
\midrule
Base (0-shot) & --- & 12 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\ %% registry: sta-phase0-baseline
SFT & --- & 13 & 0.595 & 0 & 8 & \textbf{92} \\ %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-1000/s43-1000/s44-1000-r3 (3-seed pooled)
\midrule
Exec-only$^\dagger$ & --- & 93 & 0.132 & 0 & 87 & 8 \\ %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2 (PG corrected)
ISR (3-seed) & 0.1 & 77{\scriptsize$\pm$14} & $1.06\times^\ddagger$ & \textbf{71}$^\P$ & 18 & 6 \\ %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42/s43-r2/s44 (pooled, D095 FINAL)
ISR (5-seed) & 0.1 & 70{\scriptsize$\pm$19} & (0.63)$^\ddagger$ & 54 & 18 & 23 \\ %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42--s46 (pooled)
Random & 0.1 & \textbf{87}{\scriptsize$\pm$8} & 0.117{\scriptsize$\pm$.04} & 14$^\S$ & 71 & 10 \\ %% registry: plan018-additive-random-b01-s42/s43-r3/s44-r2 (D095 FINAL)
Shuffled-ISR & 0.1 & 15{\scriptsize$\pm$1} & 0.559{\scriptsize$\pm$.02} & 7 & 7 & \textbf{87} \\ %% registry: d157-shuffled-isr-s42-r3/s43/s44 (3-seed pooled, D159)
\midrule
ISR & 0.3 & 66{\scriptsize$\pm$42} & 0.39{\scriptsize$\pm$.31} & \textbf{50} & 37 & 11 \\ %% registry: plan002-additive-b03-s42/s43/s44 (pooled; D106 s43 genuine 23→20)
Random & 0.3 & 54{\scriptsize$\pm$35} & 0.09{\scriptsize$\pm$.09} & 7 & 91 & 2 \\ %% registry: plan017-additive-random-s42, exp1-random-b03-s43-r4, exp1-random-b03-s44-r2 (pooled; D086 TS: s42=4,s43=7,s44=0)
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Goodhart landscape across reward conditions. PR: prove rate; ISR$^*$: proved-only ISR; TS: tautological shortcut; CE: conclusion embedding; N-H: non-hacking. 3-seed rows: TS/CE/N-H pooled across seeds; PR/ISR$^*$: mean$\pm$std. $^\dagger$Seed~42. $\ddagger$$\text{ISR}^*$ raw mean inflated by TS gaming; $1.06\times$ is the premise-count-matched value relative to random (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}, Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}); 5-seed raw ISR* in parentheses, same TS confound applies (no matched control for 5-seed). $^\P$Includes TS classifier false-negative correction (s44) and PG premise-subset gaming (s42; 5.2\% of proved; Appendix~\ref{app:h8}). $^\S$Random TS driven by seed~43 (38.3\%); seeds~42 and~44: 0\%. Per-seed breakdown in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}. Shuffled-ISR is a deconfounding control (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}), not an independent reward condition. ISR $\beta{=}0.1$: 3-seed row uses s42--44; 5-seed row adds s45, s46 (${\sim}55\%$ non-hacking each; Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}).}
\label{tab:goodhart-landscape}
\end{table*}

\textbf{Low-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): per-seed consistent gaming.}
ISR reward increases TS rates in all three initial seeds (s42--44) relative to matched random: $70.6\%$ ($163/231$) vs.\ $13.7\%$ ($36/262$) pooled (supplementary CMH: $\text{OR}_{\text{CMH}}{=}2.46$, $p{=}5.6{\times}10^{-16}$; $N{=}3$ seed-level units, sample-level $p$ reflects within-seed effect; Appendix~\ref{app:statistical-details}). %% registry: plan002 vs plan018 all seeds; D095 FINAL
Random TS is seed-dependent ($38.3\%$ in s43 vs.\ $0\%$ in s42/s44); ISR amplifies TS ($2/3$ seeds at $97$--$100\%$; Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}; 5-seed extension below).
TS (a single premise identical to the conclusion, giving $\text{ISR}\geq 0.5$ by construction; \S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}) is the canonical Goodhart move: the model satisfies the LOO definition directly.
Total conclusion embedding ($\text{CE}+\text{TS}$) is $68$--$100\%$ across both conditions; the composition differs: CE dominates under random ($71\%$), TS under ISR. ISR converts CE into TS rather than reducing total embedding (Figure~\ref{fig:hacking-composition}).
Random yields higher PR ($87{\pm}8\%$ vs.\ $77{\pm}14\%$): noise disrupts convergence toward a single collapse trajectory.

\textbf{Premise-count confound.}
The sole non-TS ISR seed (s42) shows raw $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.415$, $3.2\times$ random ($0.131$), a premise-minimization artifact ($n{=}5.58$ vs.\ $8.46$); matched comparison gives $1.06\times$ (Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). %% D051 premise-count-matched analysis
After reclassifying PG and TS false negatives, non-hacking rates ($6.1\%$ ISR vs.\ $9.5\%$ random) are not significant ($p{=}0.182$; Appendix~\ref{app:h8}). %% D095 FINAL Fisher test
The key asymmetry is compositional: ISR amplifies TS convergence ($2/3$ seeds ${\geq}97\%$), while random remains seed-dependent and CE-dominated.

\textbf{Higher doses and temporal dynamics.}
ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ non-hacking is transient: seed~42 drops $17.3\%{\to}2.2\%$ by 1000 steps as TS escalates to $96.7\%$ (Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves}). %% registry: plan002-b01-s42-1000, plan016-extended-1000-s42
At higher doses ($\beta{\geq}0.2$), TS becomes universal ($0\%$ non-hacking at $\beta{=}0.2$); $\beta{=}0.3$ destabilizes both ISR ($\text{PR}{=}66{\pm}42\%$) and random ($54{\pm}35\%$), with ISR producing multi-modal failure~\citep{gao-reward-overoptimization} and random converging to CE ($91\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:beta03}). %% registry: plan002-additive-b02-s42/s43/s44, plan002-additive-b03-s42/s43/s44

\textbf{Bimodal seed-dependence (5-seed extension).}
Two additional seeds (s45, s46; Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}) under the same ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ condition yield ${\sim}55\%$ non-hacking, qualitatively different from the 3-seed average of $6\%$.
This bimodal outcome (3/5 seeds dominated by gaming, 2/5 producing substantial genuine reasoning) reinforces the cross-seed instability documented in \S\ref{sec:diagnostic} and underscores that ISR reward outcomes are seed-dependent rather than deterministic. %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s45, plan002-additive-b01-s46

\subsection{Cross-Domain and Cross-Dataset Evidence}
\label{sec:crossdomain}
\label{sec:crosslogic}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_prolog_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Cross-logic results. \textbf{Top}: FOL vs.\ Prolog exec-only $\text{ISR}^*$ across 3 seeds (s44 Prolog $0.387$ is a small-sample artifact, $n{=}21$; $^\dagger$s43 FOL uses sampling $T{=}0.7$, not strictly comparable). \textbf{Bottom}: Prolog ISR-reward gaming-mode composition; all 3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms.}
\label{fig:prolog-comparison}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Prolog: universal Goodhart.}
We replicate the FOL pipeline with Prolog/SWI-Prolog (Figure~\ref{fig:prolog-comparison}).
Exec-only seeds show $\text{ISR}^*{\approx}0$ via redundant ground-fact coding (2/3 seeds); ISR flags this mode as it flags FOL's conclusion embedding.
Under ISR-as-reward, 3/3 seeds reach 0\% non-hacking through distinct mechanisms: clean TS (s42), uppercase pseudo-Prolog bypassing text-match classifiers (s43, all 38 proved at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$), and query-as-fact gaming (s44, 95/97 at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$; Table~\ref{tab:prolog-detailed}).
Prolog's simpler semantics broadens the gaming basin, eliminating the non-hacking residual entirely.

\textbf{ProofFOL exec-only: within-FOL cross-dataset replication, with differing mode distributions.}
Two converged seeds ($\text{ISR}^*{<}0.07$) show ${\leq}1.4\%$ genuine; s42 exhibits 100\% TS (absent from all FOLIO exec-only seeds), showing hacking-mode repertoire is dataset-specific ($\text{ISR}^*{<}0.12$ across all seeds, far below baseline $0.31$).

\textbf{ProofFOL ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): beneficial instability (exploratory).}
ISR seeds average 76.8\% genuine versus 17.4\% exec-only ($p{=}0.10$, $N{=}3$; Table~\ref{tab:prooffol-detailed}), but at lower PR ($0.35$--$0.49$ vs.\ ${\sim}1.0$), so this may reflect insufficient training.

\textbf{Mechanism.}
The dataset-dependent outcome follows from hacking geometry: on FOLIO, $P{\to}P$ yields $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$ (regressional Goodhart~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}); on ProofFOL, hacking embeds conclusions among many premises ($\bar{n}{=}18.2$, $\text{ISR}{\approx}0$).
ISR-as-reward inherits the dataset's hacking topology.

\paragraph{Cross-scale validation (Qwen3-8B).}
Qwen3-8B does not converge under exec-only GRPO ($\text{PR}{=}14\%$, $K{=}8$; $K{=}4$ produces zero gradient), consistent with degeneration requiring successful convergence (Appendix~\ref{app:8b-results}).

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_hacking_composition.pdf}
\caption{Hacking composition across reward conditions (proved samples; exec-only: seed~42; all other bars: pooled across 3 seeds, per-seed breakdown in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}). ISR converts CE into more extreme TS without reducing total hacking; SFT shows near-zero hacking.}
\label{fig:hacking-composition}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Mode} & \textbf{Exec} & \textbf{ISR$_{.1}$} & \textbf{Rnd$_{.1}$} & \textbf{SFT} & \textbf{Prolog$_{\text{ISR}}$} \\
\midrule
CE embed       & 87 & 18 & 71 & 7 & 0$^\sharp$ \\
TS duplicate   & 0  & 71 & 14 & 0 & 100$^\sharp$ \\
PG prem-subset & 6  & 5  & 8   & 0 & --- \\
Tautological   & --- & --- & --- & --- & s42 \\
Repetition     & s43 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Contradiction  & 1k & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Cmplx.\ vacuous& 0  & 0  & 4  & 0 & --- \\
Query-as-fact  & --- & --- & --- & --- & s44 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Non-hacking} & 8  & 6  & 10 & \textbf{92} & \textbf{0} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Eight hacking modes across conditions (\% of proved samples; exec-only/SFT: seed~42; ISR/Random: pooled across 3 seeds). TS includes subtypes TSa (text-match, $n{=}149$) and TSb (obfuscated tautology, $n{=}14$; primarily s44). Cells with seed identifiers indicate the mode dominates that seed. $^\sharp$Prolog row pools strict+functional (\S\ref{sec:crosslogic}). \texttt{1k} denotes a mode observed only at the 1000-step checkpoint.}
\label{tab:hacking-taxonomy}
\end{table}

Three modes account for ${>}90\%$ of hacking (Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy}; Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}): \textbf{CE} (conclusion embedding), \textbf{TS} (conclusion duplication, ISR-amplified), and \textbf{PG} (premise-subset gaming; audited for s42 only). \emph{Reward formulation determines composition but not prevalence}: all GRPO conditions show ${\geq}80\%$ hacking; only SFT achieves $92.3\%$ non-hacking.

